414 0 obj
<</Title(The CA-3 intervention is a deliberately constructed scheduling-policy defect. The task list contains unequal-cost work, with the first 10% of tasks assigned a higher computational cost. A contiguous block distributor assigns the first block to worker 0, concentrating most expensive tasks on that worker. Other workers can finish early while worker 0 continues, producing load imbalance and a longer overall completion time.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 361 0 R/Prev 402 0 R/Next 403 0 R>>
endobj

415 0 obj
<</Title(how work is assigned to them \(block, round-robin or dynamic distribution\). For CA-3, the failure is framed as an intentionally defective user-space task scheduler: it divides a cost-skewed task list into contiguous equal-count blocks. Equal task counts do not imply equal work. Because the first 10% of tasks are ten times more expensive, a contiguous partition places a disproportionate amount of work in the first worker's block. The other workers may finish sooner and remain idle while the slowest worker determines total completion time.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 393 0 R/Prev 404 0 R/Next 416 0 R>>
endobj

416 0 obj
<</Title(Observed failure indicators to record: high worker CPU-time imbalance, poor scaling, low effective parallel utilization, and longer wall-clock completion time than a balanced distribution. This is a workload-placement defect in the experiment's)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 393 0 R/Prev 415 0 R/Next 405 0 R>>
endobj

